rification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-11 \\\\ y+7x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The coefficient matrix is not invertible\n- The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not zero\n- The lines $x+2y=-11$ and $y+7x=4$ intersect at infinitely many  points" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e992d347e15d71ebc54717a2a152c7e98bbc5288a3c3225bbaac9a24b08514a" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e992d347e15d71ebc54717a2a152c7e98bbc5288a3c3225bbaac9a24b08514a" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5451afbc5e7a8376cec33ffd7224c0a1de532ca93c8a79fd1541bdb67ac9714f" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e992d347e15d71ebc54717a2a152c7e98bbc5288a3c3225bbaac9a24b08514a" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I  and  III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e992d347e15d71ebc54717a2a152c7e98bbc5288a3c3225bbaac9a24b08514a" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" 