r/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e992d347e15d71ebc54717a2a152c7e98bbc5288a3c3225bbaac9a24b08514a" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$ intersect at one point, then \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e992d347e15d71ebc54717a2a152c7e98bbc5288a3c3225bbaac9a24b08514a" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5451afbc5e7a8376cec33ffd7224c0a1de532ca93c8a79fd1541bdb67ac9714f" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e992d347e15d71ebc54717a2a152c7e98bbc5288a3c3225bbaac9a24b08514a" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e992d347e15d71ebc54717a2a152c7e98bbc5288a3c3225bbaac9a24b08514a" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/refere