attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is the solution to $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e992d347e15d71ebc54717a2a152c7e98bbc5288a3c3225bbaac9a24b08514a" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) =0$\n\n- The system ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/0b4ad54b702a4c7dc8cca849c32d035e558a8b91d8df22b14eb65b28f41093b0.png) has exactly one solution\n\n- $A^{-1}$ does not exist" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/example-4511.json" "file_sha256" "c1bb402f56bac7ac9640064e219ffc5e6e337ed64a0fbfa16233ae6202f78666" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/79f95782298c108b174ce190af7b3a073fa0f704072932967c4f16313c955ce7.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/example-4511.json" "file_sha256" "c1bb402f56bac7ac9640064e219ffc5e6e337ed64a0fbfa16233ae6202f78666" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/15ba3333a2ced8790efd81a35d26be43d2f0841b9529bf9d6b5b5e1c0e9f9b67.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/18978e96f80b0ba3d0f997c1db6162886b9d9e2d2c5ee7d5925a48b6147081af.png) where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n\n- The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/example-4512.json" "file_sha256" "f45bcc8e24bd051d3499e97e126072ef50853bc401fb19936a0faea36d8ccd0a" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/e49dcc022775cc8eb5317611a3fae4d21892a7c566989f67f65e5213ee5c6b9d.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =k-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4.$ \n\nSo, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/example-4512.json" "file_sha256" "f45bcc8e24bd051d3499e97e126072ef50853bc401fb19936a0faea36d8ccd0a" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/example-4513.json" "file_sha256" "e1b0220811d65b5f3cca8b4548775346e2f8f0ba2d1cd51ce61a58bb461a2ca8" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/db710af5540e8c811c83c12c23a5f5289539fc8628a936c183badc00933b2068.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/example-4513.json" "file_sha256" "e1b0220811d65b5f3cca8b4548775346e2f8f0ba2d1cd51ce61a58bb461a2ca8" "basis" 1776 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-32167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- $Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a46ee1a390280c634670a0516a4367d1318ed52ca759a92f952f27fa283a3b6" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-32167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and III give us true statements:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $det(A)\\ne 0.$* $\\,✓$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a46ee1a390280c634670a0516a4367d1318ed52ca759a92f952f27fa283a3b6" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-32167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7d3087baf55591da19e797c183909e7f6b4eb60e0a79522f295cd288037478e" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-32167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a46ee1a390280c634670a0516a4367d1318ed52ca759a92f952f27fa283a3b6" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-32167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a46ee1a390280c634670a0516a4367d1318ed52ca759a92f952f27fa283a3b6" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-32167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/refere