ademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a46ee1a390280c634670a0516a4367d1318ed52ca759a92f952f27fa283a3b6" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a46ee1a390280c634670a0516a4367d1318ed52ca759a92f952f27fa283a3b6" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}].$ If $k\\ne 1$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=1-k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point. \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,✓$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a46ee1a390280c634670a0516a4367d1318ed52ca759a92f952f27fa283a3b6" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7d3087baf55591da19e797c183909e7f6b4eb60e0a79522f295cd288037478e" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a46ee1a390280c634670a0516a4367d1318ed52ca759a92f952f27fa283a3b6" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a46ee1a390280c634670a0516a4367d1318ed52ca759a92f952f27fa283a3b6" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a46ee1a390280c634670a0516a4367d1318ed52ca759a92f952f27fa283a3b6" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution for the system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a46ee1a390280c634670a0516a4367d1318ed52ca759a92f952f27fa283a3b6" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "The lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ are parallel"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a46ee1a390280c634670a0516a4367d1318ed52ca759a92f952f27fa283a3b6" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "The system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a46ee1a390280c634670a0516a4367d1318ed52ca759a92f952f27fa283a3b6" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73608" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a46ee1a390280c634670a0516a4367d1318ed52ca759a92f952f27fa283a3b6" "basis" 2172 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065987/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-127920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=-1 \\\\ 3y-x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The lines $2x-2y=-1$ and $3y-x=1$ intersect at infinitely many  points\n- The coefficient matrix is not  invertible\n- The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not equal to zero" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb6e512bd9fd170f3727e09e4a890f1d55404ee5b6496242f073d8838bc3a71e"} {"question" "q-127920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-32091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+\\sqrt{7}y=0.3 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{0.1}x+3y=\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The lines $3x+\\sqrt{7}y=0.3$ and $-2\\sqrt{0.1}x+3y=\\sqrt{7}$ intersect exactly at one point\n- The determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $0$\n- The coefficient matrix is invertible" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-32091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-32091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb6e512bd9fd170f3727e09e4a890f1d55404ee5b6496242f073d8838bc3a71e"} {"question" "q-32091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-32091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-32091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-11 \\\\ y+7x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The coefficient matrix is not invertible\n- The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not zero\n- The lines $x+2y=-11$ and $y+7x=4$ intersect at infinitely many  points" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb6e512bd9fd170f3727e09e4a890f1d55404ee5b6496242f073d8838bc3a71e"} {"question" "q-127918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I  and  III only"] ["text" "I  only"] ["text" "II  and  III only"] ["text" "II  only"] ["text" "III  only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II  only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127919" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $A$ is  invertible\n- $Ax=0$ has a nonzero solution" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127919" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127919" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb6e512bd9fd170f3727e09e4a890f1d55404ee5b6496242f073d8838bc3a71e"} {"question" "q-127919" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127919" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127919" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-36073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has an infinite number of solutions" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-36073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-36073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb6e512bd9fd170f3727e09e4a890f1d55404ee5b6496242f073d8838bc3a71e"} {"question" "q-36073" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-36073" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-36073" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-73567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The lines $2x+2y=2$ and $3x+ky=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n- $Ax=0$ has the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-73567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $2k-6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- Statement III is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-73567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb6e512bd9fd170f3727e09e4a890f1d55404ee5b6496242f073d8838bc3a71e"} {"question" "q-73567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-73567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-73567" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-73565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The lines $2x+4y=0$ and $3x+6y=0$ intersect at exactly one point\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-73565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem: \n\n- Statement II is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-73565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb6e512bd9fd170f3727e09e4a890f1d55404ee5b6496242f073d8838bc3a71e"} {"question" "q-73565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-73565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-73565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n- The lines $x+3y=-1$ and $-x+ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k+3\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to  $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb6e512bd9fd170f3727e09e4a890f1d55404ee5b6496242f073d8838bc3a71e"} {"question" "q-127931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-127931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-47652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has no solution\n- The lines $5x-y=-1$ and $-5x+ky=0$ intersect exactly at one point" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-47652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =5k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $5k-5\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-47652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb6e512bd9fd170f3727e09e4a890f1d55404ee5b6496242f073d8838bc3a71e"} {"question" "q-47652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-47652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-47652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-128136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -4.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The system  $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- The lines $7x+14y=2$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at infinitely many points" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-128136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =7\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =7k+28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $7k+28\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -4.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-128136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb6e512bd9fd170f3727e09e4a890f1d55404ee5b6496242f073d8838bc3a71e"} {"question" "q-128136" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-128136" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-128136" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$ intersect at one point, then \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e487c4188f0864aedbb65aa9572b691b06c605d4a14a497d6855c70aab6cbe1"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb6e512bd9fd170f3727e09e4a890f1d55404ee5b6496242f073d8838bc3a71e"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "fil