reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/example-4511.json" "file_sha256" "abdc20140b7adb90b19cb14ee0cd3de5cce8349e72c2de4dc87eab0b69c8007a"} {"question" "e-4511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/assets/79f95782298c108b174ce190af7b3a073fa0f704072932967c4f16313c955ce7.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/example-4511.json" "file_sha256" "abdc20140b7adb90b19cb14ee0cd3de5cce8349e72c2de4dc87eab0b69c8007a"} {"question" "e-4512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/assets/15ba3333a2ced8790efd81a35d26be43d2f0841b9529bf9d6b5b5e1c0e9f9b67.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/assets/18978e96f80b0ba3d0f997c1db6162886b9d9e2d2c5ee7d5925a48b6147081af.png) where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n\n- The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/example-4512.json" "file_sha256" "eae45a255aaf236e05d5920da22b1b5ef52c1ba4bc8284020ad9847c74d2108b"} {"question" "e-4512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/assets/43da43be5ab47c640387f77d3b61555d79e9b9a2d61f49d00b56e45a6856bdec.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =k-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4.$ \n\nSo, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/example-4512.json" "file_sha256" "eae45a255aaf236e05d5920da22b1b5ef52c1ba4bc8284020ad9847c74d2108b"} {"questio